sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Simulated fracture of self-similar staggered composites with 1 to 4 levels" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy> ;
    dct:description "Settle the compounding question in a model where constituents are held exactly fixed, which no dataset allows. Build 2D lattice-spring (or discrete-element beam) fracture models of the tension-shear staggered architecture (Ji and Gao 2004; Gao et al. 2003): level 1 is mineral platelets in a soft matrix; level n replaces each platelet of level n-1 with a scaled-up copy of the level n-1 composite, keeping the total mineral fraction fixed. Mineral and matrix springs have fixed stiffness and brittle failure strains. Load notched specimens in mode I to compute work of fracture and the resistance curve. Vary aspect ratio and mineral fraction at each level; compare against a single-level composite with the same total mineral fraction and against a random (non-staggered) arrangement of the same phases." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Curve of work-of-fracture enhancement versus number of levels at fixed constituents with realization-level 95% CIs, showing whether enhancement is monotone, saturating or non-monotone, and its sensitivity to aspect ratio." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Results that change by more than the realization spread when specimen size or lattice resolution changes (size effect not controlled), making the levels comparison an artefact; or failure to reproduce the level-1 analytic stiffness within 5%." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Implement a 2D quasi-static lattice-spring fracture code (Python with sparse solvers), validated on the analytic tension-shear chain stiffness formula for level 1. 2. Generate hierarchical geometries for n = 1..4 at fixed total phi, with 20 random realizations each (platelet offset jitter). 3. Run notched fracture tests; record peak load, work of fracture, R-curve slope; check mesh and specimen-size convergence (specimen at least 10 times the largest level length). 4. Fit enhancement versus n and test monotonicity and saturation (isotonic versus logistic-saturation fit). 5. Write the predicted enhancement-per-level curve as the quantitative prior for approach 1's levels coefficient." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A 2D spring model omits 3D crack deflection and twisting and fibril-level plasticity; it answers the compounding question for the model, not for real tissues." ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:note "Tool: scipy-sparse" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-02> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-16> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2004.03.006> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0631609100> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/361511a0> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2006.07.007> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/biocomposite-toughness-from-hierarchy> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does hierarchy itself explain the toughness of biological composites?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2004.03.006> dct:title "Mechanical properties of nanostructure of biological materials" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Ji and Gao 2004, Mechanical properties of nanostructure of biological materials" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.0631609100> dct:title "Materials become insensitive to flaws at nanoscale: Lessons from nature" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gao et al. 2003, Materials become insensitive to flaws at nanoscale: lessons from nature" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/361511a0> dct:title "Materials with structural hierarchy" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Lakes 1993, Materials with structural hierarchy" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmps.2006.07.007> dct:title "On the mechanics of mother-of-pearl: A key feature in the material hierarchical structure" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Barthelat et al. 2007, On the mechanics of mother-of-pearl" .
}

